{
  "image_name": "02bb5b692bc978eb2483c5eca6db5ed0.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how four CRISPR–Cas surveillance systems find and destroy nucleic acid targets, highlighting both shared logic and key mechanistic differences. In all cases, a crRNA-guided protein complex first scans potential targets, then forms a short initial “seed” pairing, followed by more extensive base pairing that triggers a conformational change and nuclease activity.\n\nType I systems use a Cascade complex to recognize a DNA target next to a PAM, recruit Cas3, and then degrade the target through combined nuclease and helicase action. Type II systems use Cas9 with tracrRNA/crRNA; after PAM recognition and seed pairing, Cas9’s own nuclease domains are activated to cut the DNA, after which other DNases appear to finish degradation. Type III-A systems use a Csm complex, apparently targeting nucleic acid through initial pairing but also checking for non-repeat sequence identity so that repeat-derived sequences do not activate the system; after full pairing, a putative nuclease/helicase (labeled Csm6?) or other nucleases degrade the target. Type III-B systems use a Cmr complex to target RNA rather than DNA, with cleavage carried out by a nuclease subunit of the complex and then further RNA degradation.",
